<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Truth and Reconciliation Commission (South Africa)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1995">1995</a>–<a href="/years/2002">2002</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truth-and-reconciliation-commission-peru">Truth and Reconciliation Commission</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> was set up in 1995 after the end of apartheid to investigate gross human rights violations committed between 1960 and 1994. Chaired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desmond-tutu">Archbishop Desmond Tutu</a>, it held public hearings from 1996 in which victims testified and perpetrators could receive amnesty if they made full disclosure of politically motivated crimes. Its report, presented from 1998, became a model for truth commissions elsewhere.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The commission was set up under the Promotion of National Unity and Reconciliation Act, No. 34 of 1995, and was based in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a>. Authorised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nelson-mandela">Nelson Mandela</a>, it was a court-like body of restorative justice, charged with bearing witness to and recording human rights violations, granting amnesty in some cases, and offering reparation and rehabilitation to victims. A register of reconciliation let ordinary South Africans express regret for past failures.</p><p>Besides Tutu, its members included Alex Boraine as deputy chairman, Sisi Khampepe, Wynand Malan, Klaas de Jonge and Emma Mashinini. Tutu enriched the mandate with the African concept of Ubuntu, which in Xhosa expresses the idea that together people make one another human.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The work was divided among three committees. The Human Rights Violations Committee investigated abuses between 1960 and 1994, the Reparation and Rehabilitation Committee was charged with restoring victims’ dignity and proposing ways to help them, and the Amnesty Committee considered applications from individuals. Public hearings were held at many venues, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannesburg">Johannesburg</a> and Randburg. They were at first to be held in camera, but 23 non-governmental organisations won media access, and on 15 April 1996 the national broadcaster televised the opening of the first hearing live.</p><p>Amnesty could be granted where crimes were politically motivated, proportionate and fully disclosed. To avoid victor’s justice, no side was exempt from appearing: the commission heard both the apartheid state and the liberation movements, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">African National Congress</a> among them. It found 7,000 political deaths under apartheid between 1948 and 1989, 73 of them in detention in the hands of the security police, and more than 19,050 victims of gross human rights violations. Of 7,111 amnesty applications, 849 were granted and 5,392 refused.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>’s first coalition government chose forgiveness over prosecution and reparation over retaliation, in contrast to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> trials, and opinions differ on how well this restorative approach worked. A 1998 survey of several hundred victims found that most felt the commission had failed to bring reconciliation between the black and white communities. Critics such as Madeleine Fullard and Mamphela Ramphele argued that its definition of gross violations left out systemic wrongs such as forced removals and pass arrests.</p><p>Many black South Africans were angered by amnesty for abuses committed by the apartheid government, and the family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steve-biko">Steve Biko</a> challenged the commission in the highest court as unconstitutional. Urgent interim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a> were made available only in 2003, five years after the commission recommended them. The Institute for Justice and Reconciliation was established in 2000 as its successor.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Truth and Reconciliation Commission (South Africa)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Truth and Reconciliation Commission</b>, chaired by Archbishop Desmond Tutu from 1996, became a model studied by many societies emerging from conflict.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">Republic of South Africa · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…UDF, and Winnie Madikizela-Mandela, banished to a small town in the Free State from 1977 to 1985, kept her husband’s name before the world, though the <b>Truth and Reconciliation Commission</b> later held her politically and morally accountable for crimes committed by her bodyguards, the Mandela United Football Club.</blockquote><a href="/movement/anti-apartheid-movement">Anti-apartheid movement · People</a></li></ul></section>
